        'content_html' => <<<'HTML'
<p>In many schools, lesson plans live in one document, assessments on another platform, grades in the student information system and communication with families in messaging groups. The result is duplicated work and nobody with a complete picture of learning. <strong>EduNova</strong> is Grupo Logic’s AI-powered learning system that brings that whole cycle together in eight connected modules, from the textbook to the report card, with a space for teachers, school leaders, students and families. Here I’ll explain how it works and suggest concrete ideas for making the most of it in the classroom.</p>

<h2>What is EduNova?</h2>
<p>EduNova is an AI-powered web platform that integrates a school’s teaching and learning work: each module feeds the next. The textbook guide serves as the basis for the lesson plan; the lesson plan generates the class presentation; assessment feeds into grading; the results point to what needs remedial work; and it all ends in a report card that families can understand.</p>
<p>It runs in the browser on computers, tablets and phones, with nothing to install. If what you’re after is rather a set of standalone AI tools for everyday work, the option from the same company is <a href="/aulamagica-ia-herramientas-ia-docentes/">AulaMágica IA</a>.</p>

<h2>What problem does it solve at school?</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>Scattered information.</strong> Planning, assessments, grades and communication live in different tools, and nobody sees the full picture.</li>
<li><strong>School leaders who find out too late.</strong> They need to know in time which grades, subjects or campuses need support.</li>
<li><strong>Teachers with no time.</strong> Hours of planning and marking eat into the time for supporting students.</li>
<li><strong>Families who don’t understand the report card.</strong> They want to know clearly how their children are progressing and how to help at home.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Main features: eight connected modules</h2>
<h3>1. Textbooks</h3>
<p>Content by subject, grade and term, aligned with Colombia’s basic competency standards and DBA (“Basic Learning Rights,” the national learning expectations by grade), with teaching sequences, readings, exercises and challenges by level.</p>
<h3>2. Planning</h3>
<p>The AI suggests objectives, lesson stages, activities and resources. Everything is editable and can be exported to Word or PDF in your school’s format.</p>
<h3>3. Lessons</h3>
<p>Presentations generated from the lesson plan, live questions and participation boards. It works with limited connectivity.</p>
<h3>4. Accessibility</h3>
<p>Easy-read versions, visual supports, narrated audio and suggestions for reasonable adjustments based on the PIAR (Colombia’s individual adjustment plan for students with disabilities).</p>
<h3>5. Assessments</h3>
<p>Quizzes, worksheets and practice tests structured like the national standardized exam (Saber, administered by ICFES, Colombia’s national testing agency), question banks by competency with different difficulty levels, and rubrics for open-ended questions.</p>
<h3>6. Grading</h3>
<p>Assisted marking of open-ended and handwritten answers, with personalized feedback that the teacher reviews and approves.</p>
<h3>7. Remedial work</h3>
<p>Identifies the learning that needs reinforcing and offers catch-up activities tailored to each student, with progress tracking.</p>
<h3>8. Communication</h3>
<p>Easy-to-understand report cards, scheduled newsletters and weekly summaries for families, with recommendations for supporting learning at home.</p>

