<h2>Ideas for using EduNova in the classroom</h2>
<p>The beauty of EduNova lies in the connections between modules. That’s why several of these ideas span more than one.</p>

<h3>1. 7th-grade natural science: from the textbook to a lesson on the water cycle</h3>
<p>Start from the textbook guide for your term and ask for a lesson plan on the water cycle with a simple hands-on activity. Adjust the timing to your actual class period, swap the materials for the ones you have in the lab and generate the presentation from that same plan. One single workflow, with no copying and pasting between programs.</p>

<h3>2. 5th-grade math: equivalent fractions with live questions</h3>
<p>Use the equivalent fractions guide as a starting point and launch live questions during the lesson. The participation board shows you right away whether the class has understood or whether you need to go back to hands-on materials. It’s formative assessment without waiting for Friday’s quiz.</p>

<h3>3. 11th grade: practice tests structured like the national exam</h3>
<p>Build practice tests from the question bank by competency, mixing difficulty levels. After the test, instead of handing back just a score, look at which competencies the group found hardest and devote the next session to those points.</p>

<h3>4. 9th-grade language arts: assisted marking of handwritten answers</h3>
<p>Give a reading comprehension worksheet with open-ended questions on paper and use assisted marking with a clear rubric. The platform suggests the feedback; you review it, adjust it and approve it before it reaches the student.</p>

<h3>5. End of term: a tailored remedial plan</h3>
<p>Instead of the classic one-size-fits-all “make-up worksheet,” use the remedial module to identify the learning each student needs to reinforce and assign personalized catch-up activities. Progress tracking tells you who has already overcome the difficulty.</p>

<h3>6. 6th-grade social studies: the same reading, accessible to everyone</h3>
<p>If there are students with a PIAR in your class, generate the easy-read version of the reading on natural regions, with visual supports and narrated audio. That way the whole class works on the same content and nobody is left out of the discussion.</p>

<h3>7. Homeroom teachers: weekly summaries and newsletters for families</h3>
<p>Schedule newsletters for the key dates of the term and rely on the weekly summaries so families know what was covered and how to help at home. A well-informed parent or guardian comes to report card day with questions, not surprises.</p>

<h3>8. Academic coordination: early warnings for the department meeting</h3>
<p>With the indicators dashboard by grade, subject and campus (included in the Volume License), check mid-term where low-performance alerts are showing up. Bring that data to the department meeting and agree on actions before the problem turns into students failing the subject.</p>

<h3>9. Campuses with poor connectivity: lessons that don’t depend on a perfect network</h3>
<p>The lessons module works with limited connectivity. If you teach at a rural campus or in a classroom where the signal drops, generate the presentation from your lesson plan ahead of time and walk into class with the lesson ready.</p>

<h3>10. Academic council: your own textbooks with EduNova Studio</h3>
<p>With EduNova Studio (included in the Volume License), the school can create its own textbooks reflecting its teaching model, its PEI (the school’s institutional educational project), its colors and its logo, in digital format and also ready to print, and update them every year. It’s a great project for each department to write the content for a grade based on its own experience.</p>

